  "limitations": [
    "Candidate: public build adbb7a2 (rc2), a sanitized export of SpaceApp stable 77d00f6895 (the 1.1.7 release-train candidate) with the 1.1.7 overlay commits: OpenCode 2 (@opencode/cli 2.0.32) as the first CLI, the MIT licence, integrity pins, the consistent backup, the Codex sessions mount and the review fixes listed in the 1.1.7 CHANGELOG. rc2 differs from rc1 (8ab12f2) only in OpenCode's own update check, which is off in the CLI image (OPENCODE_DISABLE_AUTOUPDATE=true in the CLI wrapper and the two OpenCode helpers): on rc1 OpenCode 2.0.32 opened an 'Update available' dialog over a new pane that took the first prompt (VM219).",
    "Images tested by ID from the candidate archive (images-1.1.7.tar.gz sha256 757190809cc2f23571c4d6f6f433fe33a7a7d4e800373d8c35add64b4931ce8a): core sha256:df544d7778942d66127469f925af194d220301b87e040d5fd5f22c6b2e64fac3 and browser sha256:dd51214961ebca9350165585a32dd2f9b64895f075dce0655bf10306defc39fb were not rebuilt for rc2 (rc1's images; their only difference to an adbb7a2 build is the three OpenCode files under /app/deploy/docker, which only the CLI container runs); cli sha256:e04e9e6f8dd357447bcf1d0cde9b5228d4411f1d1631a99ed903ab22a9075ff7 differs from rc1's CLI image only in those three files and /usr/local/bin/spaceapp-cli-wrapper (file-by-file sha256 diff). The release workflow builds all three images from adbb7a2.",
    "VM hosts are x86_64. ARM64 container validation remains a release workflow gate.",
    "Fresh installer timings use preloaded local candidate images; they exclude network image downloads.",
    "The 1.1.6 -> 1.1.7 update ran on each retained original installation, rolled back to 1.1.6 before each full session.",
    "Windows desktop commands ran as interactive scheduled tasks in an owned RDP session; this is not keystroke coverage of every wizard prompt.",
    "No known exceptions. Every first-agent check and every Getting Started first prompt ran on OpenCode 2 with OpenCode Zen's free model opencode/big-pickle, from the lab's own address 162.55.99.104 with no proxy variables, and completed with real tool calls (read, write, shell, webfetch) and no permission prompt. macOS (vm221): the first attempt (02:03-02:08 UTC on 2026-10-21) submitted the task and OpenCode opened its session (ses_ea3c90544ffeEhLaqAo5s5Jo0F), but no answer arrived within the check's limit and no reason was recorded; the session resumed on the same candidate after a VM restart and the task completed (02:17-02:20 UTC). Both attempts are kept in the lab evidence (vm221-try1).",
    "rc2 fix check on every system: the Getting Started pane and the first-agent pane were watched for 120-124 s after the composer was ready; OpenCode showed no update dialog and the first prompt reached the pane (the macOS first attempt recorded no watch).",
    "The Zen exception code from the 1.1.7 candidate (scripts/release-evidence.mjs, label zen-refusal-1.1.7) is unused: this file names no known exception, so the release check verifies first-agent strictly on every platform. It stays in the 1.1.7 tree because removing it would change the tested candidate; the 1.1.8 export removes it.",
    "Every result in this file was executed on candidate adbb7a2 (source 77d00f6895): Linux vm218, Windows vm219 and macOS vm221 main sessions and the small/medium/large profiles of all three. rc1 (8ab12f2) results are not counted.",
    "Every AI check ran on OpenCode's free model through OpenCode Zen; no paid model and no lab key was used.",
    "Codex on public installs: the start without Codex's own update menu and the turn state in the room monitor are covered by the candidate's unit tests (codex-public-update-prompt, codex-home-public-install, codex-sessions-mount); pane start not exercised on the VM219 lab: no Codex sign-in (placeholder credential, no turn). VM219's backup/restore with Temporal ran on a medium-profile fixture (the small profile has no Temporal): writers paused, temporal and temporal_visibility dumps and the CLI settings in the backup, marker and settings back after restore.",
    "Install telemetry Yes ran on Linux only, on a separate owned fixture purged afterwards; Telemetry No on all three systems: no install id, no marker, and no lab event at the collector over each No fixture's life.",
    "Profiles ran one VM boot per sequence under the 20 GiB host memory floor (large profiles on 16 GiB VMs, started only with at least 30 GiB available on the host); every runner was reverted to 8 GiB/4 CPU.",
    "Automatic browser opening was evidenced on Linux (lab Chrome CDP page list right after the install) and macOS (console capture); Windows ran the install with open=yes in the signed-in desktop, but no desktop capture was taken.",
